<header class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Local draft review</p><h1>Find the next edit.</h1><p class="lead">Use the article outline and writing-pattern suggestions to make a deliberate review easier. Your judgment still decides what belongs in the page.</p></header>
<h2 id="preview-passages">Go from a reading passage to its source</h2>
<p>Open the reading preview and turn on Find passages in editor. Choose Edit beside a paragraph, heading, list group, quotation, code block, or table to select its exact Markdown in the article field. Repeated passages have separate controls with their source line numbers. A list group or table is selected as a whole, including its Markdown markers. Links keep opening normally; selecting text in the preview still works.</p>
<p>Edit the selected source, then choose Return to this passage in preview below the article. The return follows the current editor cursor in the updated article, so inserted or removed paragraphs can change which passage is shown. Navigation itself keeps writing and export approval unchanged. Actual edits clear approval through the existing editor. Apply or clear a pending import before using these controls.</p>
<p>Controls cover the first 200 passages; the full article stays available to read and edit. Passage navigation is temporary, starts off, and is excluded from backups and all exports. It changes focus and selection only. It does not edit automatically, save writing, or make a network request.</p>
<h2 id="source-review">Review a claim against its source</h2>
<p>In the workspace, select a short, specific passage in the article body and choose Review a selected claim. Or open Review claims against sources and enter its exact text. The comparison shows your current source notes; you can also refer to a document or source you checked elsewhere. Select a short source excerpt and choose Use selected source text as evidence, or explain the evidence yourself.</p>
<p>Add a source reference and choose Needs checking, Supported by this source, Needs revision, or Remove or qualify. Supported requires both a reference and evidence. Other resolved decisions require an explanation. This records your judgment only; no source or URL is opened, and nothing establishes that a claim is true. Recheck decisions after editing the article or source. Exact matching is a navigation aid, not a verification result.</p>
<p>Save private review note adds a readable record to the existing private review notes. Edit review note reopens a saved record without changing it until Update. The combined record has a 500-character limit, shared with ordinary notes, and all notes together are limited to twelve. Field limits are 240 for the passage, 160 for its source reference, and 240 for evidence. Shorten the record if needed; rejected selections and oversized pastes are kept out in full. The tool does not truncate them. Markup and references remain literal text.</p>
<p>Saving keeps a checkpoint before changing notes and clears export approval. Saved records are included in explicit private backups, checkpoint downloads, and collection entries after Add or Update. All article exports exclude them. Review fields that have not been saved are temporary and excluded from files. Importing an article, restoring writing, loading the fictional sample, or clearing the desk discards this unsaved preparation. Discard this unsaved review leaves saved notes and approval unchanged.</p>
<p>When the article or source notes change during preparation, saving stops until you compare the current writing and choose Recheck against current writing. A saved record can select its passage only when it occurs exactly once in the current article. Missing or repeated text is disclosed without guessing an occurrence. Review a record through its guided editor, or edit/remove its underlying text in Review questions &amp; notes. A manually changed record that no longer follows the guided format remains a normal private note.</p>
<h2>Run a review</h2><ol><li><a href="/write/">Open your article in the workspace</a> and expand Outline &amp; draft checks.</li><li>Select Check my draft. It inspects the current title and body locally, without AI or a network request.</li><li>Use Edit line to select the passage in the article field. Edit it yourself, or keep it when the suggestion does not apply.</li><li>Run the checks again after editing. Previous findings and their text positions are cleared when the article changes.</li></ol>
<h2>What is checked</h2><ul><li>Possible unfinished text such as TODO, TBD, TK, [insert a detail], and template braces.</li><li>Repeated paragraphs of at least eight approximate words, ignoring capitalization and whitespace.</li><li>Empty headings or sections, skipped heading levels, and a body heading that repeats the title field.</li><li>Prose paragraphs over 120 approximate words. This is an editorial suggestion, not a rule that every paragraph must satisfy.</li><li>Unclosed fenced code and inline links or images that remain text in the reading-page export.</li><li>Generic web-link labels such as click here.</li></ul><p>Code blocks are excluded from the prose checks. Inline code is excluded from placeholder and word counts. The parser handles a limited Markdown subset; nested markup, reference links, tables, and other formats can be missed. Intentional repetitions and ordinary words can be flagged. At most forty findings and forty outline/link entries are shown at once.</p>
<h2>Navigate and estimate</h2><p>The outline follows Markdown headings in the current body. Selecting an entry highlights its source text. Approximate word counts include headings and link labels, but exclude code, image syntax, and link addresses. Reading time assumes 200 words per minute; it is an estimate, not a measured reading time, and word splitting is less informative for some languages.</p>
<h2>Reorder sections</h2><p>Move up and Move down swap a section with its adjacent sibling at the same heading level and within the same parent. A section includes all deeper headings and their writing until the next heading at its own or a higher level. The opening passage before the first heading stays in place. Line breaks between sections and at the end of the article keep their original positions; section writing is not rewritten.</p><p>A move saves the previous workspace in Revisions in this tab, clears export approval, and clears outdated draft findings. You can restore the checkpoint to undo it. Moves stop while a file is being read, if a checkpoint cannot be saved, or if a code fence is unfinished. Only moves among the first forty displayed headings are available. Underlined headings and headings inside code are outside this outline. Read the preview after moving, especially when your destination supports additional Markdown features.</p>
<h2>Review the link handoff</h2><p>Simple inline Markdown such as <code>[A useful resource](https://example.com/)</code> becomes a link in the preview and HTML reading page. Only absolute http:// and https:// addresses without embedded credentials are supported. Labels are rendered as text. Links open a new tab with referrer and opener protection; nothing is loaded until a reader selects the link.</p><p>Images, source HTML, relative addresses, fragments, reference-style links, email links, and unsupported URLs remain text in the reading page. Use Links &amp; image handoff to locate recognized inline syntax, then adapt unsupported material in your destination. Page Markdown preserves the authored body for its own renderer. These checks never visit addresses or establish that they are safe, available, or accurate sources.</p>
<h2>Keep the judgment with you</h2><p>A result with no flagged patterns is not certification. Compare claims, names, dates, quotations, uncertainty, and permissions with your actual source. Check the downloaded file and destination preview. <a href="/guides/review/">Use the human review checklist</a>.</p><p>Checks never rewrite or approve the article. Check results and outline state are temporary and excluded from workspace backups and article exports. Copy check summary explicitly copies the findings, not source notes or private review notes; clipboard handling belongs to your device.</p><p><a class="button" href="/write/">Review your article</a> · <a href="/help/markdown-export/">Choose your export</a></p>
